1
00:00:00,069 --> 00:00:03,297
Yesterday we asked Bahn
fourteen hard questions.

2
00:00:03,347 --> 00:00:04,695
What hurts most?

3
00:00:04,745 --> 00:00:06,223
Where do things go wrong?

4
00:00:06,273 --> 00:00:08,874
Their answers changed
our product overnight.

5
00:00:08,924 --> 00:00:12,126
This is the Bahn control room, and
today it's built around what Bahn

6
00:00:12,176 --> 00:00:13,376
actually told us.

7
00:00:13,426 --> 00:00:16,560
Out of a hundred live orders,
fourteen need a person.

8
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:17,797
Not hundreds.

9
00:00:17,847 --> 00:00:20,552
Tens, exactly the volume Bahn asked for.

10
00:00:20,602 --> 00:00:24,070
And they're sorted by the real deadline,
so the most urgent one is always

11
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:25,241
on top.

12
00:00:25,841 --> 00:00:27,215
Bahn's worst nightmare?

13
00:00:27,265 --> 00:00:30,073
A car they could deliver,
that never arrives.

14
00:00:30,123 --> 00:00:33,812
A driver picks it up, goes
silent, and messages days later.

15
00:00:33,862 --> 00:00:36,046
So that's now our number one signal.

16
00:00:36,096 --> 00:00:38,546
This BMW was picked up in Hamburg.

17
00:00:38,596 --> 00:00:41,532
No position, no message,
for thirteen hours.

18
00:00:41,582 --> 00:00:42,921
We don't wait days.

19
00:00:42,971 --> 00:00:45,768
We flag it in hours,
with one clear action:

20
00:00:45,818 --> 00:00:49,252
phone the driver now, and here's
exactly what happens if they don't

21
00:00:49,302 --> 00:00:50,341
answer.

22
00:00:50,929 --> 00:00:52,951
And we listened on GPS too.

23
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:56,573
Bahn said signal drops all the
time in the German countryside.

24
00:00:56,623 --> 00:00:59,652
So a tracking gap alone
barely moves the score.

25
00:00:59,702 --> 00:01:02,453
Silence plus no progress after pickup?

26
00:01:02,503 --> 00:01:04,155
That's when a person gets called.

27
00:01:04,204 --> 00:01:08,182
At six hours, the agent quietly
checks in with the driver first,

28
00:01:08,232 --> 00:01:09,712
all by itself.

29
00:01:10,317 --> 00:01:12,987
Bahn told us the latest
arrival time is soft.

30
00:01:13,037 --> 00:01:15,024
One late car isn't a crisis.

31
00:01:15,074 --> 00:01:17,859
So this Ferrari, forty-three minutes late,

32
00:01:17,909 --> 00:01:19,294
stays with the agent.

33
00:01:19,344 --> 00:01:21,424
It's this customer's first late car.

34
00:01:21,474 --> 00:01:23,727
But when the same dealer
keeps getting late cars,

35
00:01:23,777 --> 00:01:26,854
that's lost trust, and
it escalates to you.

36
00:01:27,485 --> 00:01:30,166
Every Bahn driver is
trained to drive any car.

37
00:01:30,216 --> 00:01:32,990
So we threw out our
value-based driver tiers.

38
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,969
This Bentley keeps its driver.

39
00:01:35,019 --> 00:01:38,395
No more swapping people just
because the car is expensive.

40
00:01:38,445 --> 00:01:40,617
We score reliability instead:

41
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:43,848
on-time rate, responsiveness,
and the record.

42
00:01:44,473 --> 00:01:46,078
Where does it hurt on the road?

43
00:01:46,128 --> 00:01:49,469
Bahn said Germany and
Travemünde, and winter tyres.

44
00:01:49,519 --> 00:01:53,080
Now every route that touches
Travemünde is watched automatically,

45
00:01:53,130 --> 00:01:54,793
with time for port check-in.

46
00:01:54,843 --> 00:01:58,694
The map shows risk, caution and
opportunities along the way.

47
00:01:58,744 --> 00:02:02,571
And when the legal winter season
starts, a winter tyre check switches on

48
00:02:02,621 --> 00:02:03,927
by itself.

49
00:02:04,521 --> 00:02:05,605
Pickup sites?

50
00:02:05,655 --> 00:02:09,020
Bahn says the trouble is
random, no repeat offenders.

51
00:02:09,070 --> 00:02:12,040
So we judge every order
on its own evidence.

52
00:02:12,090 --> 00:02:15,235
When a dealer can't find the
car, you don't just get wait.

53
00:02:15,285 --> 00:02:17,087
You get four real options:

54
00:02:17,137 --> 00:02:19,876
wait one more hour,
release and reschedule,

55
00:02:19,926 --> 00:02:22,422
give the driver a nearby job, or cancel.

56
00:02:22,472 --> 00:02:25,445
Each one says exactly what happens next.

57
00:02:26,057 --> 00:02:28,935
Every fact has a source, in plain words.

58
00:02:28,985 --> 00:02:30,348
No record codes.

59
00:02:30,398 --> 00:02:31,369
Just:

60
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:33,519
who said it, when, and what to do.

61
00:02:33,569 --> 00:02:36,134
The agent reads, asks
the driver and the site,

62
00:02:36,184 --> 00:02:38,183
and only then brings you one decision.

63
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:40,787
It never contacts a customer on its own.

64
00:02:40,837 --> 00:02:44,215
Customer messages are drafted
for a person to send.

65
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:45,813
It's live.

66
00:02:45,863 --> 00:02:49,054
Real accidents on the German
autobahn, real weather,

67
00:02:49,104 --> 00:02:54,204
real Finnish road data, the
Helsinki to Tallinn timetable.

68
00:02:54,254 --> 00:02:59,123
And one port strike becomes
one grouped decision,

69
00:02:59,173 --> 00:03:01,450
not four separate alarms.

70
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,579
For electric cars, winter range is
clearly labelled as an estimate,

71
00:03:03,629 --> 00:03:04,429
because Bahn told us they
don't have that data yet.

72
00:03:04,833 --> 00:03:08,083
Ops can switch the whole room
to Finnish in one click.

73
00:03:08,133 --> 00:03:09,043
Next up:

74
00:03:09,093 --> 00:03:12,642
the weekly caught versus missed
report Bahn's management asked for.

75
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:14,552
Bahn never had a risk assessor.

76
00:03:14,602 --> 00:03:16,403
Now they have one that listens.

77
00:03:16,453 --> 00:03:21,023
Fewer cars lost, fewer surprises,
and every decision explained.
